\contentsline {comment}{Commented\nobreakspace {}(ALB): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{conforming?}}{2}{section*.3}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(ALB): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ We adopt $C^0$-elements with an interior penalty approach to weakly enforce gradient continuity across interior mesh interfaces (see \cite {Brenner}). This results into a non-conforming Discontinuous Galerkin (DG) method\nobreakspace {}\cite {Arnold82, Arnold01} for our fourth-order problem formulated in $H^2$. A comprehensive comparison of DG methods for fourth-order problems can be found in \cite {Arnold00}, while standard\comment [id=ALB]{conforming?} implementations for biharmonic problems are discussed in \cite {Georgoulis08, Dong23}. For the specific case of von Kármán equations, $C^0$-DG methods and corresponding error estimates are provided in \cite {Carstensen}. Our implementation builds upon the formulation in \cite {brunetti}, but instead of embedding the penalty terms into the discrete weak form, we generalise the approach by incorporating them directly into the energy functional. }}{2}{section*.4}%
\contentsline {added}{Added\nobreakspace {}(ALB): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{ Discontinuous Galerkin methods are non-conforming finite element methods, which rules out restricting the energy functional $\mathcal {\setbox \z@ \hbox {\mathsurround \z@ $\textstyle F$}\mathaccent "0365{F}}$ to a finite-dimensional subspace of $H^2_0(\Omega ) \times H^2_0(\Omega )$ to derive the discrete weak formulation of problem \eqref {2408121717}. Following \cite {Brenner:Von_Karman}, a proper DG formulation requires adding additional terms to the energy functional. These terms account for discontinuities in the gradient across mesh interfaces and ensure the coercivity of the problem. Let $\mathcal {T}$ denote a regular triangulation of $\Omega \subset \mathbb {R}^2$. Define \( \mathcal {E}_{\text {bd}}\) as the set of boundary edges, \( \mathcal {E}_{\text {in}}\) as the set of internal edges, so that \( \mathcal {E} := \mathcal {E}_{\text {in}}\cup \mathcal {E}_{\text {bd}}\) is the collection of all edges. For $T \in \mathcal {T}$, let $\mathrm {P}_3(T)$ be the space of polynomials of degree at most three defined on $T$. The Lagrange finite element space $\mathcal {V}(\mathcal {T})$ consists of globally continuous, piecewise cubic polynomials that vanish on the boundary edges \( \mathcal {E}_{\text {bd}}\), namely }}{2}{section*.5}%
\contentsline {comment}{Commented\nobreakspace {}(ALB): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{unclear def. $\pm $ if T on the boundary}}{2}{section*.6}%
\contentsline {comment}{Commented\nobreakspace {}(ALB): \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{is K the triangle T?}}{2}{section*.7}%
